Camoufox: WebRTC host candidates follow the identity's interfaces, not the host's.

Firefox gathers one host address per family on the machine's primary interface
(without a capture: the default IPv4 address and the IPv6 address on the same
interface), so the number of host candidates, their local preferences (the
interface prioritizer puts IPv6 first: 0x7F, then IPv4 0x7E) and which one the
srflx belongs to describe the machine's network. A dual-stack home machine shows
two addresses with the srflx on the IPv4 one; Camoufox on a host without IPv6
(a server, a container) showed one, at 0x7F, whatever machine the identity is.
Measured on a Windows 11 machine with IPv4 and router-advertised ULAs on one
adapter: 2 UDP + 2 TCP host candidates, srflx 0x7e1f, in-browser loopback
connected; on a host without IPv6: 1 + 1, srflx 0x7f1f.

With webrtc:hostipv6, content appends ::1 under the primary IPv4 interface's
name to the STUN addresses (NrIceStunAddr::ClaimedHostIPv6) unless the host has
a global IPv6 address of its own. nICEr keeps a loopback address on an interface
that also has a real one (nr_stun_is_claimed_host_addr), which no host reports,
and uses it as the default IPv6 address when the host has no IPv6 route. ::1
pairs only with loopback candidates, so a connection inside the browser
succeeds over it, as a real machine's does over its own IPv6, and nothing
leaves the machine. Host candidates carry mDNS names, so a page never sees the
address.

A srflx Camoufox makes up when none formed (behind a proxy that carries no
UDP) takes the priority nICEr would give it: its IPv4 base's interface
preference (0x7E next to an IPv6 address, 0x7F alone), and that host's port.
It had 0x7F1F fixed, and the first UDP host's port, which with an IPv6 address
on the interface is the IPv6 one's.

